{"tool_name":"bacello","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"bacello","home":"https://busca.biocomp.unibo.it/bacello","summary":"Predictor for the subcellular localization of proteins in eukaryotes.","addition_date":"2015-01-22T11:31:34Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-24T14:07:43.705191Z","tool_type":["Web application"],"version":["1.0"],"collections":["Bologna Biocomputing Group"],"accessibility":"Open access","biotools_curie":"biotools:bacello","cost":"Free of charge","credit":[{"email":"castrense.savojardo2@unibo.it","name":"Castrense Savojardo","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0002-7359-0633","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Maintainer"]},{"email":"pierluigi.martelli@unibo.it","name":"Pier Luigi Martelli","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0274-5669","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]},{"email":"andrea@biocomp.unibo.it","name":"Andrea Pierleoni","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]},{"name":"ELIXIR-ITA-BOLOGNA","typeEntity":"Division","typeRole":["Provider"],"url":"http://biocomp.unibo.it"}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"https://busca.biocomp.unibo.it/bacello"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"input":[{"data":{"term":"Sequence record","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_0849"},"format":[{"term":"FASTA","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_1929"}]}],"note":"Prediction for the subcellular localization","operation":[{"term":"Protein subcellular localisation prediction","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_2489"}],"output":[{"data":{"term":"Report","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_2048"},"format":[{"term":"HTML","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_2331"}]}]}],"maturity":"Mature","name":"BaCelLo","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"ELIXIR-ITA-BOLOGNA","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/bioinformatics/btl222","metadata":{"abstract":"Motivation. The knowledge of the subcellular localization of a protein is fundamental for elucidating its function. It is difficult to determine the subcellular location for eukaryotic cells with experimental high-throughput procedures. Computational procedures are then needed for annotating the subcellular location of proteins in large scale genomic projects. Results. BaCelLo is a predictor for five classes of subcellular localization (secretory pathway, cytoplasm, nucleus, mitochondrion and chloroplast) and it is based on different SVMs organized in a decision tree. The system exploits the information derived from the residue sequence and from the evolutionary information contained in alignment profiles. It analyzes the whole sequence composition and the compositions of both the N- and C-termini. The training set is curated in order to avoid redundancy. For the first time a balancing procedure is introduced in order to mitigate the effect of biased training sets. Three kingdom-specific predictors are implemented: for animals, plants and fungi, respectively. When distributing the proteins from animals and fungi into four classes, accuracy of BaCelLo reach 74% and 76%, respectively; a score of 67% is obtained when proteins from plants are distributed into five classes. BaCelLo outperforms the other presently available methods for the same task and gives more balanced accuracy and coverage values for each class. We also predict the subcellular localization of five whole proteomes, Homo sapiens, Mus musculus, Caenorhabditis elegans, Saccharomyces cerevisiae and Arabidopsis thaliana, comparing the protein content in each different compartment. \u00a9 2006 Oxford University Press.","authors":[{"name":"Casadio R."},{"name":"Fariselli P."},{"name":"Martelli P.L."},{"name":"Pierleoni A."}],"citationCount":300,"date":"2006-07-15T00:00:00Z","journal":"Bioinformatics","title":"BaCelLo: A balanced subcellular localization predictor"},"pmid":"16873501","type":["Primary"]}],"topic":[{"term":"Model organisms","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0621"},{"term":"Model organisms"